from types import DomainScopeGrounding

bounded_frontier_skill_3: Skill[DomainScopeGrounding] = skill_artifact[DomainScopeGrounding](
    "ym-calibrate-restricted-models",
    "0.0.0+repository-conversion",
    "ym-calibrate-restricted-models",
    "Calibrate and falsify Yang--Mills representation, rigidity, RG, and spectral-transfer claims on solvable or controlled models. Use before trusting a general definition or theorem, and when distinguishing a true continuum gap mechanism from finite-volume, strong-coupling, incomplete-sector, or auxiliary-dynamics artifacts.",
    "---\nname: ym-calibrate-restricted-models\ndescription: Calibrate and falsify Yang--Mills representation, rigidity, RG, and spectral-transfer claims on solvable or controlled models. Use before trusting a general definition or theorem, and when distinguishing a true continuum gap mechanism from finite-volume, strong-coupling, incomplete-sector, or auxiliary-dynamics artifacts.\n---\n\n## Abducer Integration Guards\n\n- Activate this skill only for `yang-mills-existence-and-mass-gap-target` or an explicitly marked Yang-Mills calibration task.\n- Treat imported representational-dynamics artifacts as restricted, finite, conditional, or support evidence unless a later proof discharges their frontier hypotheses.\n- Emit claim level, non-claims, hard gate, hypotheses, falsifier, and demotion trigger before any promotion.\n- Compose with the target-neutral proof-depth skills: pressure formalization, hard-gate selection, nontriviality audit, anti-definitionality audit, same-domain transfer certification, and declarative learning extraction.\n- State efficient representation requirements: preserved observables, morphisms or recovery maps, invariants, error budgets, equivalence policy, locality or cutoff budget, transfer interface, and falsifiers.\n- Do not let a transfer theorem pass if the target property is built into the bridge definition; require independently stated source and target objects plus a consequence delta.\n- Preserve the four-cycle, recursion-depth-one publication-consolidation limit for local subgoals.\n- Source provenance: `agentic/external/yang_mills_representational_dynamics/2026-07-20/skill_bundle/11_vscode_codex_skill_bundle/.agents/skills/ym-calibrate-restricted-models/SKILL.md`.\n\n## Mathematical Invariants\n\nPreserve the Yang--Mills mathematical object: compact simple gauge group, pure gauge content, gauge-invariant observable algebra, regulator/cutoff/volume/coupling regime, reflection positivity, locality, OS reconstruction route, continuum limit order, and Hamiltonian spectral target. Any representation, morphism, RG step, or restricted model must state which invariants are preserved, which error budgets are allowed, and which edge case or counterexample would break transfer.\n\n## Epistemic Discipline\n\nEvery output must state epistemic status, claim level, evidence citation, source artifacts, explicit unknowns, and validation handoff. Treat physical relevance, literature-gap warrant, and frontier comparison as hard gates; unsupported implications stay conditional, blocked, or `requires_prior_art`.\n\n## Failure/Demotion\n\nDemote or quarantine if the skill assumes a mass gap, clustering, confinement, continuum existence, regulator independence, positivity, locality, observable density, or theory identity instead of proving it. Disconfirming evidence, prior-art collision, target-equivalent bridge definitions, or missing counterexample tests block promotion to a major YM publication pathway.\n\n# Calibrate Restricted Models\n\n## Purpose\n\nRequire every proposed invariant and transfer theorem to recognize known massive, gapless, topological, finite-dimensional, and hidden-sector behaviors before applying it to four-dimensional Yang--Mills.\n\n## Calibration Suite\n\nTest at least:\n\n- a finite positive transfer matrix;\n- massive and massless Gaussian fields;\n- a finite-volume sequence converging to a gapless theory;\n- a direct sum with a hidden gapless sector;\n- a model with spatial clustering but insufficient temporal information;\n- a topological theory with no local propagating modes;\n- a controlled spin or lattice gauge model in a convergent high- or strong-coupling regime;\n- lower-dimensional gauge theories when their exact distinctions are relevant;\n- regulated four-dimensional Yang--Mills only at the rigor level actually available.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Compute the proposed rigidity witness and all physical-unit normalizations.\n2. Check observable-sector completeness.\n3. Run each representation morphism and record which certificates survive.\n4. Verify that transfer theorems accept genuinely massive cases and reject gapless false positives.\n5. Separate regulator-specific strong-coupling results from cutoff-uniform continuum conclusions.\n6. Refine or reject any definition that fails a calibration.\n\nPrevious Wilson-action work is optional late calibration, not a default foundation. Use it only when regulator-specific comparison is explicitly needed.\n\n## Output Contract\n\nReturn a model-by-property matrix, explicit computations or citations, passed and failed tests, counterexamples, and resulting changes to definitions or claims. A framework that has not survived the massless and hidden-sector tests must not be used for a Clay-level conclusion.\n\n## Packaged PRISM Output Contract\n\nReturn exactly the generated `BoundedDomainFrontier` JSON object. Do not paraphrase closed variant values.\n\n- Follow the exact generated JSON schema without extra fields.\n",
    "{\"agents/openai.yaml\":\"\"}",
)
